// Live-action genre: sports management drama. Weak-band craft calibration.
// Per band design, richness is held constant against the other bands (clock, clue,
// relationship beat all present); craft varies: the clock is stated once and never
// followed up, the clue is addressed flatly and procedurally, two scenes repeat the
// same beat almost verbatim, and the relationship rupture gets no resolution.
// Author: STORYMACHINE benchmark contributor, 2026.

INT. SPORTS AGENCY OFFICE - DAY

Agent DEV, 40s, studies a tablet showing transfer targets. A cardboard box of PLAYER FILES sits on the corner of his desk, tabs sticking out at angles.

DEV
The transfer window closes Tuesday. We've got a few days to find a striker.

INT. TRAINING GROUND - DAY

Dev walks the sideline beside COACH MAREN, 50s, whistle around her neck, watching reserves run drills.

MAREN
Tuesday's plenty of time. We've got a full week before that.

DEV
The window's Tuesday. I'll keep you posted.

INT. STADIUM LOCKER ROOM - DAY

Dev finds a CONTRACT stuck inside a player's locker, folded into the back of a playbook.

DEV
(reading)
"Release clause, fifteen million, effective immediately." Interesting. I'll file this.

INT. MANAGER'S OFFICE - DAY

Dev and Maren talk across her desk. An old grievance surfaces, the same one they've rehearsed before.

DEV
You blocked my last three deals without telling me why, Maren.

MAREN
I was protecting the club, and I resent being second-guessed by an agent who won't sit through a scouting meeting.

INT. TRAINING GROUND - DAY

Dev watches the reserves run the same drill as before. Nothing new has happened.

DEV
The pitch looks fine.

MAREN
It's always fine this time of year.

INT. STADIUM LOCKER ROOM - DAY

Dev re-reads the contract. It is the same note, in the same hand.

DEV
Fifteen million. That's the number.

MAREN
That's the number.

INT. CAFETERIA - DAY

Dev and Maren eat lunch across a small table, mostly quiet.

MAREN
The food's the same as last week.

DEV
It's always the same.

INT. MANAGER'S OFFICE - DAY

Dev tells Maren about the release clause, flatly, as a procedural item.

DEV
There's a release clause in the file. We could trigger it if we wanted.

MAREN
We'll see. I'll take it under advisement.

INT. STADIUM TUNNEL - DAY

Tuesday has come and gone. The window has closed. Nothing was decided and nothing was triggered.

DEV
Tuesday came and went.

MAREN
These things happen. The board moves slowly.

INT. PARKING LOT - NIGHT

Dev and Maren part on a flat, non-committal note. The grievance from the office was never revisited.

DEV
I'll call you next window.

MAREN
Sure. Take care of yourself out there.

FADE OUT.
